      <p>
        Because Christmas Day, the weekend, and the Boxing Day substitute all fall together in 2026 (Fri–Mon), anyone with a payment due anywhere in that window should expect it earlier than usual — often several days before Christmas — and a longer-than-normal wait before the next one. GOV.UK publishes confirmed exact dates closer to the time; check there if your specific date isn't listed above.
      </p>

      <h2>State Pension: payment day depends on your National Insurance number</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike most other benefits, the State Pension isn't paid on a fixed date — it's paid every 4 weeks, on a day of the week set by the last two digits of your National Insurance number:
      </p>
      <div class="bpd-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Last 2 digits of NI number</th>
              <th>Payment day</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>00–19</td><td>Monday</td></tr>
            <tr><td>20–39</td><td>Tuesday</td></tr>
            <tr><td>40–59</td><td>Wednesday</td></tr>
            <tr><td>60–79</td><td>Thursday</td></tr>
            <tr><td>80–99</td><td>Friday</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        If your usual payment day falls on a bank holiday, the same early-payment rule applies — you'll be paid on the last working day before.
      </p>

      <h2>Universal Credit: same date every month</h2>
      <p>
        Universal Credit is paid once a month, on the same date each cycle, based on when you made your claim. If that date falls on a weekend or bank holiday, it's brought forward to the last working day before — but your <strong>assessment period</strong> (which determines your award amount) doesn't change, only when the money physically lands.
      </p>

      <h2>What to do if your payment hasn't arrived</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>First, check whether your due date fell on a weekend or bank holiday — if so, it was likely paid early, so check your account from the working day before.</li>
        <li>Allow one extra working day for your bank to process it.</li>
        <li>If it still hasn't arrived, contact the relevant office (DWP for most benefits, the Pension Service for State Pension, HMRC for Child Benefit).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bpd-faq">
        <p>
          <strong>If my payment is paid early because of a bank holiday, do I get less next time?</strong>
          No — the amount you're entitled to doesn't change. You just get it slightly sooner this cycle, and the gap before your next payment will be a little longer than usual.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why did my Scotland-based friend's payment not change for a Scottish bank holiday?</strong>
          Scotland-only bank holidays (2 January, St Andrew's Day, and Scotland's earlier Summer bank holiday) don't affect DWP payment dates, because the Bacs payment system continues operating on those days. Only UK-wide or England/Wales/NI bank holidays typically move payment dates for Scottish claimants.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Does this apply to Child Benefit too?</strong>
          Usually, yes — but not always in the same direction. Child Benefit is administered by HMRC, and in some cases (particularly around Northern Ireland-only bank holidays) it has arrived a day later rather than earlier. Check your specific date if you're unsure.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>I get paid on the 1st of the month — what happens over New Year?</strong>
          If your payment date is 1 January, you'll be paid on the last working day before — 31 December — since New Year's Day is a UK-wide bank holiday.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Does an early payment affect my other benefits or Universal Credit assessment period?</strong>
          No. Early payment is purely about when the money arrives in your account; it doesn't change your entitlement, your assessment period, or your award amount.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where can I find exact confirmed payment dates for a specific month?</strong>
          GOV.UK publishes exact confirmed dates for bank holidays and any payment adjustments, usually well in advance. This page will be updated whenever new dates are confirmed, but for the most time-sensitive periods (like Christmas), it's worth double-checking closer to the date.
        </p>
      </div>
